14:50:27 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re often right: many free apps do **not** make much money, especially small or niche ones. A few reasons they can still exist: - **The creator may be making little or nothing.** Many apps are hobby projects, portfolios, school projects, or something the developer built for themselves. - **Costs can be low.** A simple app with a few thousand users may cost only a modest amount to host and maintain, especially if it mostly runs on the user’s phone. - **Ads may be subtle or only shown sometimes.** For example, an app may show an ad only after certain actions, only in certain countries, or only to non-paying users. - **A small number of users can matter.** If an app has hundreds of thousands or millions of users, even a low ad rate or a 1–3% premium conversion rate can become meaningful. - **The app may support another business.** A free calculator, tracker, or tool might bring people to a company that sells a paid product, service, course, or enterprise version. - **It may collect revenue indirectly.** Some apps use affiliate links, sponsored recommendations, paid integrations, or partnerships rather than obvious ads. - **The creator may be building reputation.** A successful free app can help someone get freelance work, a job, investors, customers, or attention for future products. - **It may be funded externally.** Open-source projects and public-interest apps can be supported by donations, grants, a university, a nonprofit, or a company. Also, app-store downloads can be misleading. A free app with 100,000 downloads might have only a small number of active users, and after Apple/Google’s fees, taxes, advertising rates, hosting, support, and development time, its creator may not be earning a full-time income at all. So the short answer is: the apps with barely any ads are often either making money in a less visible way—or not making much money at all.
