14:50:49 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Yes — that broadly backs up what you’re saying. The app market is very uneven: a small number of developers and companies earn enormous amounts, while most independent developers earn little or lose money after expenses. Exact percentages vary by study, year, and whether it counts all developers or only active commercial apps, but the general pattern is well established. A typical small free app may have: - few active users, - low ad revenue per user, - almost no paid upgrades, - ongoing costs for hosting, tools, support, and updates, - lots of unpaid development time. For example, even if an app earns a few hundred dollars per month from ads, that may be nowhere near enough to cover the creator’s time—let alone provide a salary. Meanwhile, a game or social app with millions of active users can make far more than thousands of small apps combined. So “free app creator” does not automatically mean “profitable business.” For most, it is a side project, marketing tool, learning experience, portfolio piece, or an attempt to eventually reach the small group of apps that do become financially successful.
